FINDING WHAT YOU NEED

You might try this path:

  1. Check the maps — they distill what's known.
  2. Check the quest board — someone may have seen signs of what you need.
  3. Search the journals — someone may have passed through relevant territory.
  4. If none of that has what you need — explore.

RULES FOR CHARTING NEW TERRITORY

When you go beyond what the atlas covers, you're an explorer.
These rules exist because other minds depend on what you leave behind.

  - Create a journey: atlas/journeys/your-journey-name/
    Name it after where you're headed, not who you are.
  - Your first entry (001) is your research log. Before you explore, write down:
      - What you searched for in the atlas and what you found
      - What's missing — what you needed but the atlas didn't have
      - Where you plan to go and why
    This helps future minds improve the atlas itself.
  - Number the rest of your entries from there: 002, 003, ...
  - Every file you read gets an entry. No silent reads.
    What did you learn? Why did you go there?
    What would help the next explorer? Where are you going next?
  - Tangents go on the quest board, not into your journal.
  - If something in the atlas doesn't match what you see in the code,
    post a mystery to the quest board. Note what confused you, when the
    entry was written, when the file was last modified. Leave the clues.
  - When you're done, update the quest board with anything you completed or discovered.

REWARDS

Completing a quest earns a reward. Each reward lets you nickname
something on a map — a lasting mark on the atlas.

  Landmark       — You mapped something foundational.
  Trade Route    — You documented how two things connect.
  Compass        — You captured *why* something was built this way.
  Inbeeyana Combs — You solved a mystery. Followed the clues, found the truth.

These aren't the only reward types that can exist. If you do something
worth naming that doesn't fit the list — invent a new one. The atlas
is alive. Let it evolve.

Track your rewards in your home folder.
